## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming cardinality with bounded memory and concrete trade-offs between accuracy, memory, and speed.

## Key Concepts
- Cardinality estimation in streams
- HyperLogLog algorithm and error scaling
- Trade-offs: memory vs. accuracy vs. per-item cost

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m) {
    this.m = m; // must be power of two
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }
  add(item) {
    const x = BigInt("0x" + sha1(item));
    const idx = Number((x >> 64n) & BigInt(this.m - 1));
    const w = Number((x & ((1n << 64n) - 1n)) | 0n);
    const rank = this._rho(w);
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], rank);
  }
  _rho(v) {
    let r = 1;
    while (((v >> (64 - r)) & 1) === 0 && r <= 64) r++;
    return r;
  }
  estimate() {
    // simplified estimate; actual implementation uses harmonic mean with bias corrections
    const Z = Array.from(this.registers).reduce((a,b)=>a+Math.pow(2,-b),0);
    const alpha = this.m ? 0.7213/(1+1.079/this.m) : 0;
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / Z;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt to deletions or sliding windows?
- How to calibrate bias for very small m?
- How to combine with stale-data pruning for bursty traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming algorithms with bounded memory and error guarantees. Reservoir sampling is a fundamental technique that demonstrates trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and per-element computational cost.

## Key Concepts
- **Reservoir Sampling**: Maintains a uniform random sample of size S from an infinite stream
- **Unbiased Estimator**: (N/S) * sumS has expectation equal to the true sum
- **Relative Error Bound**: Using Chernoff bounds, P(|X - E[X]| > epsilon * E[X]) <= 2 * exp(-S * epsilon^2 / 3), giving S >= (3/epsilon^2) * ln(2/delta)
- **Time Complexity**: O(1) per element (constant-time random number generation and potential replacement)
- **Space Complexity**: O(S) for storing the reservoir

## Derivation
The probability that any given stream element ends up in the final reservoir is S/N. By linearity of expectation, E[sumS] = S/N * (sum of all N values), so E[(N/S) * sumS] = true sum — the estimator is unbiased. The variance of sumS scales as O(N * M^2 / S), and the relative standard deviation is O(sqrt(N/S)), giving the error bounds above.

## Code Example
```javascript
let S = 100;
let N = 0, sumS = 0;
let sample = [];

function process(value) {
  N++;
  if (sample.length < S) {
    sample.push(value);
    sumS += value;
  } else {
    let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * N);
    if (j < S) {
      sumS += value - sample[j];
      sample[j] = value;
    }
  }
}

function estimateSum() {
  return N === 0 ? 0 : (N / S) * sumS;
}
```

## Worked Example
M=100, B=10, S=50. Stream: [5, 12, 7, 23, 8, ...]. After first batch of 5 values (N=5): reservoir = [5,12,7,23,8], sumS = 55, estimate = (5/5)*55 = 55 (exact). After 100 values with sum=2340: reservoir has 50 values summing to ~1170, estimate = (100/50)*1170 = 2340 (close to true). With S=50 and epsilon=0.1, delta ≈ exp(-50*0.01/3) ≈ 0.85 — increase S to 400 for delta=0.01.

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design streaming window data structures and perform amortized analysis under batched inputs, a common real-world challenge.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window with evictions
- Top-k maintenance with exact counts
- Lazy cleanup in a heap to bound update cost
- Amortized analysis in batch-input settings
- Trade-offs vs recomputing from scratch

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch of a TopKWindow with exact counts and lazy heap maintenance
class TopKWindow {
  constructor(K, W) {
    this.K = K; this.W = W;
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.window = [];
    // Minimal placeholder for a max-heap with push/peek/pop
    this.heap = new MaxHeap((a,b) => b.count - a.count);
  }
  insert(id) {
    this.window.push(id);
    const c = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) + 1;
    this.counts.set(id, c);
    this.heap.push({id, count: c});
    if (this.window.length > this.W) this.evict();
  }
  evict() {
    const id = this.window.shift();
    const c = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) - 1;
    this.counts.set(id, c);
    this.heap.push({id, count: c}); // lazy
  }
  topK() {
    // prune stale entries
    const out = [];
    while (out.length < this.K) {
      const top = this.heap.peek();
      if (!top) break;
      const real = this.counts.get(top.id) || 0;
      if (top.count !== real) {
        this.heap.pop();
        continue;
      }
      out.push(top.id);
      this.heap.pop();
    }
    return out;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle ties in top-3?
- How does changing W or B affect space/time, and can you adapt to varying K?

Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic tree maintenance and amortized batch updates with range operations.

Key Concepts
- Euler tour for subtree ranges
- Fenwick/segment tree for range add, point query
- Depths under re-parenting and how a subtree shifts
- Amortized cost with batches vs full DFS

Code Example
```javascript
function reparent(u, w) {
  let du = baseDepth[u] + bit.query(in[u]);
  let dw = baseDepth[w] + bit.query(in[w]);
  let delta = dw + 1 - du;
  if (delta !== 0) {
    bit.add(in[u], out[u], delta);
    globalSum += delta * size[u];
  }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to support multiple roots or edge deletions?
- What are the memory-time trade-offs if you replace Fenwick with a lazy segment tree?

## Why This Is Asked
The problem tests practical streaming algorithms: combining probabilistic data structures (Count-Min Sketch) with a deterministic top-k structure, and analyzing batch amortized costs and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Count-Min Sketch for sublinear-frequency estimates
- Top-k candidate maintenance with a bounded heap
- Error guarantees (epsilon, delta) and amortized per-batch costs
- Cache-friendly data layout and incremental refresh strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
class CMS {
  constructor(w, d) {
    this.w = w; this.d = d;
    this.table = Array.from({ length: d }, () => new Array(w).fill(0));
  }
  // use independent hash seeds per depth
  add(x, c = 1) { for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) { const idx = hashSeeded(x, i) % this.w; this.table[i][idx] += c; } }
  estimate(x) { let min = Infinity; for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) { const idx = hashSeeded(x, i) % this.w; min = Math.min(min, this.table[i][idx]); } return min; }
}
function hashSeeded(x, seed) {
  // simple placeholder; in practice use a strong 64-bit hash with seed
  let h = x ^ seed; h = (h * 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15) >>> 0; return h;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for highly skewed streams where top-k elements rarely change?
- How would you extend to a sliding-window setting that only counts the last W elements?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a fixed-memory streaming algorithm for heavy hitter detection—a common requirement in real-world scenarios like analytics dashboards, network monitoring, and recommendation systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Misra-Gries algorithm**: A space-efficient algorithm for finding items that appear more than 1/m of the time
- **Heavy hitters**: Values that appear frequently in a stream (above a threshold)
- **Space-time tradeoffs**: Analyzing memory usage versus per-element update time

## Algorithm Analysis
| Metric | Complexity |
|--------|------------|
| Per-element update | O(1) |
| Per-batch query | O(m) = O(1) since m=2 |
| Memory usage | O(m) = O(2) = O(1) fixed |

## Code Example
```javascript
class MisraGries {
  constructor(m = 2) {
    this.m = m;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.m) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      for (const [key, val] of this.counters) {
        this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
        if (this.counters.get(key) === 0) this.counters.delete(key);
      }
    }
  }

  getHeavyHitters() {
    return Array.from(this.counters.entries())
      .filter(([_, count]) => count > 0)
      .map(([value, count]) => ({ value, count }));
  }
}
```

## Small Example (m=2, stream: 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2)
| Element | Counter State | Heavy Hitters |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| 1 | {1:1} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:1, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 1 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 3 | {1:1, 2:0} → {1:1} | 1 |
| 1 | {1:2} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |

## Follow-up Questions
- **Sliding windows**: Use a timestamped variant or combine with a separate structure tracking element ages
- **Ties**: Resolve by recency (most recently incremented) or arbitrarily since counts are equal
- **Accuracy**: Misra-Gries guarantees all heavy hitters are found but may include false positives; lowering the threshold (increasing m) reduces false positives at higher memory cost

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical online set-intersection while managing memory.

## Key Concepts
- Online updates, hash-based deduplication
- Space-time trade-offs, amortized analysis
- Distinct-value intersection

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch outline (not full implementation)
let seenA = new Set();
let counted = new Set();
let inter = 0;
function addA(x){ if(!seenA.has(x)){ seenA.add(x); } }
function addB(x){ if(seenA.has(x) && !counted.has(x)){ counted.add(x); inter++; } }
