- What are worst-case memory bounds if streams use all M values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive amortized costs for dynamic arrays and relate growth strategies to real hardware effects.

## Key Concepts

- Amortized analysis
- Geometric series and resizing costs
- Growth factor impact on performance
- Cache locality during bulk copies

## Code Example

```javascript
let cap = 1, n = 0, arr = new Array(cap);
function append(x) {
  if (n === cap) {
    const newCap = cap * 2;
    const tmp = new Array(newCap);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) tmp[i] = arr[i];
    arr = tmp; cap = newCap;
  }
  arr[n++] = x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure the actual runtime and memory in a real language? 
- How does adaptive growth (non-constant factor) affect cache misses during resizes?

## Why This Is Asked

This question blends sliding-window technique with binary search to analyze a threshold problem, testing practical algorithm design and reasoning about complexity.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window for fixed-length subarray sums
- Binary search over the answer space
- Time/space trade-offs and edge-case handling
- Practical optimizations (early exit, optional prefix sums)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLWithSumAtMost(A, T) {
  const n = A.length;
  const ok = (L) => {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < L; i++) sum += A[i];
    if (sum <= T) return true;
    for (let i = L; i < n; i++) {
      sum += A[i] - A[i - L];
      if (sum <= T) return true;
    }
    return false;
  };
  let lo = 1, hi = n, ans = 0;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (ok(mid)) { ans = mid; lo = mid + 1; } else { hi = mid - 1; }
  }
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would the solution change if elements could be negative?
- What are the memory/speed trade-offs if you precompute prefix sums?
- How would you adapt for multiple thresholds T1..Tk in parallel?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests online order-statistics handling with batched updates and amortized reasoning; demonstrates a practical, provable structure.

## Key Concepts
- Order statistics with incremental data
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Two-heap data structure (k smallest vs the rest)
- Space vs time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class KthSmallest {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.L = new MaxHeap(); // holds k smallest
    this.R = new MinHeap(); // holds the rest
  }
  insertBatch(arr) {
    for (const x of arr) this.R.push(x);
    while (this.L.size() < this.k && !this.R.empty()) this.L.push(this.R.pop());
    while (!this.R.empty() && this.L.top > this.R.top) {
      const a = this.L.pop();
      const b = this.R.pop();
      this.L.push(b);
      this.R.push(a);
    }
  }
  kth() { return this.L.top; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How handle duplicates or dynamic k?  
- What are alternatives (order-statistics tree, Fenwick) and trade-offs?  
- How does complexity change if B ≫ k?

